592 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 122 0 R
/T (complete, average and single give very high silhouette scores \(0.5 to 0.68\), which looks great at first, but look at the cluster sizes: the biggest cluster has 3700+ customers out of 3815 and the smallest ones have only 1 to 8 customers. So these methods just pick a few extreme customers and put them in their own tiny clusters, and everyone else is in one giant cluster. The high silhouette is misleading here, and this is not a useful segmentation.)
/E (complete, average and single give very high silhouette scores \(0.5 to 0.68\), which looks great at first, but look at the cluster sizes: the biggest cluster has 3700+ customers out of 3815 and the smallest ones have only 1 to 8 customers. So these methods just pick a few extreme customers and put them in their own tiny clusters, and everyone else is in one giant cluster. The high silhouette is misleading here, and this is not a useful segmentation.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [67]
>>
endobj
593 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 122 0 R
/T (ward has a lower silhouette \(about 0.26 to 0.29\) but its clusters are much more balanced, because it tries to minimize the variance inside clusters \(the same idea as K-Means\).)
/E (ward has a lower silhouette \(about 0.26 to 0.29\) but its clusters are much more balanced, because it tries to minimize the variance inside clusters \(the same idea as K-Means\).)
/Pg 24 0 R
